name:
Practical (code): The XOR MLP below (layers 2 → 8 → 1, ReLU hidden layer, linear output, mean squared error, full-batch gradient descent) is written in PyTorch and in JAX. Describe the model and the training procedure the code implements, and explain how the two versions differ in how parameters, gradients and updates are expressed. (Flax NNX is a third, class-based JAX version.)
<PRE>
# PyTorch
model = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(2, 8), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(8, 1))
# He initialization of weights, zero biases (omitted)
loss_fn = nn.MSELoss()
opt = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.1)
for _ in range(2000):
    opt.zero_grad()
    loss = loss_fn(model(X), y)
    loss.backward()
    opt.step()

# JAX (params = list of (W, b) pairs)
def mlp(params, x):
    for W, b in params[:-1]:
        x = jax.nn.relu(x @ W + b)
    W, b = params[-1]
    return x @ W + b
def loss(params, X, y):
    return jnp.mean((mlp(params, X) - y) ** 2)
@jax.jit
def step(params, X, y, lr=0.1):
    grads = jax.grad(loss)(params, X, y)
    return jax.tree.map(lambda p, g: p - lr * g, params, grads)
for _ in range(2000):
    params = step(params, X, y)
</PRE>
Data: X holds the four binary input pairs and y the XOR targets 0, 1, 1, 0.
